  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 129 PM CDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nRest of Today - Tonight: Rain Continues this Afternoon and \nEvening, Exits to the East Overnight  \n\nRain will continue to pivot through the region throughout the \nafternoon and into the evening hours. Early this afternoon the \nsurface cyclone responsible for our precipitation is currently \ncentered over portions of southwest MN and northwest IA with the\ndry slot associated with it pushing into portions of northeast \nIA and southeast MN. As we continue through the afternoon, this \nsystem is expected to move generally to the southeast through \nIowa which will then allow for the deformation band associated \nwith this surface low to move overhead. Within this regime, \nsloping frontogenesis as a result of a TROWAL region will pivot \nover the region and reinvigorate shower development through the \nremainder of the afternoon and evening, particularly near and \nsouth of I-90. The main question that remains is if any \ninstability can push northward into the local area as this \nstronger forcing regime moves overhead. There is some indication\nin the recent RAP that we could build in 100-250 J/kg of MUCAPE\nin portions of southwest WI and northeast IA later this \nafternoon and into the evening. Consequently, have noted that \nsome of the CAMs are a bit more aggressive with developing \nrobust convection in these respective areas. \n\nOverall, expected additional rainfall amounts will be greatest near \nand south of I-90 where this aforementioned forcing regime is \npresent this afternoon and evening. Consequently, the 31.12z HREF \nhas an inter-quartile range of anywhere from 1 to 2 inches of \nrainfall where this forcing is present. The 90th percentile members \nof the HREF suggest some pockets of 3-4+\" may be possible, most \nlikely with any areas that see convection repeat over the same \nlocations. However, seeing this higher amounts are yet again \ncontingent on if we can manage to get any instability northward into \nthe region to promote convection. In these localized cases, cannot \nrule out some isolated flooding, however this would be\nrelatively sparse in nature. Eventually, as we head into the \novernight and daybreak on Saturday, the broader surface low and \nassociated upper-level trough will continue to push east of the\nlocal area, which in turn will note a west to east progression \nof the broader precipitation shield, likely exiting much of the \nlocal area by late morning. \n\nThis Weekend: Drier & Slightly Cooler Than Normal \n\nAs the aforementioned surface low pressure center pushes east of the \narea, upper-level ridging will build in behind it for the weekend \nwith attending surface high pressure pushing into the region later \nSaturday and into Sunday. Consequently, expecting skies to clear \nthroughout the day on Saturday with no chances for precipitation \nafter the morning hours. Additionally, with flow switching to \nnortherly over the weekend, expecting temperatures to remain \nslightly cooler than normal with the inter-quartile range in the NBM \nkeeping high temperatures across the area in the middle 70s to lower \n80s. \n\nEarly Next Week: Precipitation Chances Return Tuesday & Wednesday\n\nBy the time the beginning of the upcoming work week rolls around, \nthe previously mentioned upper-level ridging and attending surface \nhigh pressure remains in place for Monday. However, low-level flow \neventually switches to southerly which will allow for a warmer \nairmass to push into the region. Consequently, temperatures will \nlikely nudge upwards a few degrees on Monday with highs in the lower \nto middle 80s for most. Going into Tuesday, a pronounced upper-level \ntrough pushes through the Dakotas with an associated boundary \npushing southeast towards the local area. Consequently, the grand \nensemble (GEFS/EC/Canadian ensemble) has increased \nprobabilities (30-70%) for measurable rainfall Tuesday evening \nthrough Wednesday morning as it progresses through the region. \nOverall, probabilities for severe weather with this wave appear \nrelatively low as the grand ensemble (GEFS/EC/Canadian ensemble)\nonly has around 5-10% chance for ample instability and bulk \nshear to be present over the region for severe weather.",
